Serves: every scale fixture, Grimoire report, and runtime observation has one
interpretation.
Absent: a tensor-byte projection can be mistaken for peak RSS, or a native
allocator observation can be mistaken for a deterministic regression gate.
Known by: named static reports, resource kinds, scenario axes, collection
boundaries, and the conditions under which a field is exact or diagnostic.
Files: docs/benchmarks.md.
Do: distinguish `bytes` moved, `memory-bytes` resident or charged, scry-owned
logical high-water bytes, final embedding storage, requested tensor bytes, and
native working-set/private-memory observations. Define the identity of the
inference microbatch and padded sequence axes separately from the fixed v1
artifact identity. State that a sum of tensor sizes is not a peak-live claim
without an explicit liveness schedule, and that RSS/private memory remains
 diagnostic rather than a CI gate.
Accept: the document names one owner, unit, basis, collection boundary, and
comparison rule for every new field; no field can be read as a portable process
memory promise; the existing v1 baseline update rule remains coherent.
> Added the memory-accounting contract to docs/benchmarks.md: Grimoire shape-derived projections, typed memory-bytes charges, scry-owned logical bytes, and native process diagnostics now have separate bases and comparison rules.
> The fixed baseline-v1.json remains the v1 gate; future memory scenarios belong to a separate deterministic artifact, while RSS/private memory remains diagnostic.
