[scale] Reproduce the large-origin baseline (complete)
Build a deterministic local scale workload that exposes the large-origin
embedding regime before changing the production path.
Serves: later changes are compared against the same source bytes, tokenizer
tokens, span count, padded inference shape, and process boundary.
Absent: a small ten-span workload can pass while the failure only exists when
one origin creates hundreds or thousands of spans.
Known by: a local fixture or exact fixture provenance, source bytes, tokenizer
tokens, spans, candidate microbatch count, final vectors, add outcome, and the
native-memory sampling command and conditions.
Files: crates/scry-benchmarks/src/scale.rs,
crates/scry-benchmarks/src/main.rs, crates/scry/src/slice.rs,
crates/scry/src/benchmark.rs, performance.md.
Do: keep the measured input local and deterministic rather than fetching the
issue URL during a gate. Reproduce the reported regime as closely as the pinned
tokenizer permits, record the actual bytes/tokens/spans and padded sequence
length, and run the current one-call path in the process whose peak native
memory can be sampled. Compare candidate batch sizes only after the baseline
has been captured; keep setup, model loading, and artifact serialization out of
the add boundary.
Accept: the scale command reports the fixed input identity and exact logical
relationships, distinguishes one fastembed API call from its inferred internal
batches, records native metrics with their platform and sampling protocol, and
